+**Ray Casting Step:**
+During rendering, for a ray sample at position $\mathbf{p} = (x, y, z)$:
+1. **Coordinate Transformation**: Map $\mathbf{p}$ to the volume's local coordinate system. Let the integer grid indices be $i = \lfloor x \rfloor, j = \lfloor y \rfloor, k = \lfloor z \rfloor$.
+2. **Fractional Offsets**: Compute fractional parts $fx = x - i, fy = y - j, fz = z - k$.
+3. **Weight Calculation**: Compute the cubic B-spline basis function weights $w_i(fx), w_j(fy), w_k(fz)$. The 1D cubic B-spline basis function $B(x)$ is:
    $$
-   B_3(x) = \frac{1}{6} \begin{cases} 
-   (2 - |x|)^3 - 4(1 - |x|)^3 & \text{if } 1 \le |x| < 2 \\
-   2 - 6|x|^2 + 3|x|^3 & \text{if } |x| < 1 \\
+   B(x) = \begin{cases} 
+   \frac{2}{3} - |x|^2 + \frac{1}{2}|x|^3 & 0 \le |x| < 1 \\
+   \frac{1}{6}(2 - |x|)^3 & 1 \le |x| < 2 \\
    0 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
    $$
-   
-   The pre-filtering operation is effectively a deconvolution. In practice, this is often implemented as a separable 1D filter applied along each axis. For a 1D signal $v$, the pre-filtered value $v'$ at index $i$ is computed using a weighted sum of neighbors. A common approximation for the inverse cubic B-spline filter coefficients $c_k$ involves solving the system defined by the kernel's frequency response. The coefficients for the 1D pre-filter are typically:
